The problem was all of my own making in as much as I had not correctly
understood the original posted solution.  Dr Tom replied and clarified the
situation.  To quote:

"From: Dr. Thomas.Blinn@Compaq.com [mailto:tpb@doctor.zk3.dec.com] 
Sent: 01 November 2001 16:29

You misunderstood the fix.  As root, cd to /usr/sys/conf

There is a file there called "files".  There is a directory called "alpha",
in which there are several files whose names begin with "files".

Find ALL the references to "ee.mod" in those files.  Get rid of the ones put
there by the defective patch kit (which are in the "/usr/sys/conf/files"
file).  Then your kernel will
build."

Essentially I deleted the following lines:
#
# Intel 82558/82559 Ethernet driver (ee)
#
MODULE/STATIC/ee                optional ee device-driver Binary
io/dec/netif/ee.mod             module ee

from the end of the "files" file and all works as promised.


best regards
Tony Miller


